In Python, `end` is a parameter of the `print()` function that specifies what Python should display
at the end of the printed output.

### Example

```
print("Hello", end=" ")print("World")
```

Output:

```
Hello World
```

Here, `end=" "` adds a space after `Hello` instead of moving to a new line.

### Common `end` values

| Code       | Meaning                       |
| ---------- | ----------------------------- |
| `end="\n"` | Moves to a new line (default) |
| `end=" "`  | Adds a space                  |
| `end=""`   | Adds nothing                  |
| `end="-"`  | Adds a hyphen                 |

Remember: `end` controls what happens immediately after a `print()` statement finishes displaying its text.


